Project Parameters

In your SSIS project, remove these four parameters:

CES_ConnectionString
IFS_ConnectionString
CES_Password
IFS_Password

Create these six Project Parameters:

Parameter	Data type	Sensitive
CES_SQL_Server_Name	String	No
CES_SQL_Login	String	No
CES_SQL_Password	String	Yes
IFS_ORACLE_Server_Name	String	No
IFS_ORACLE_Login	String	No
IFS_ORACLE_Password	String	Yes

I strongly recommend marking both password parameters Sensitive = True.

Do not put actual passwords into the DTSX file.

2. Important Oracle detail

There is one important point with the name IFS_ORACLE_Server_Name.

Your current package contains this Oracle connection information:

HOST=ocipp-dqcof-scan.ttcociclientsub.ttcocivcn.oraclevcn.com
PORT=1521
SERVICE_NAME=ifsread1cs_svc.int.ttc.ca

The original ODBC connection therefore contains considerably more information than just a server/host name.

For the new design, I recommend setting:

IFS_ORACLE_Server_Name

to the complete Oracle Easy Connect identifier, for example:

ocipp-dqcof-scan.ttcociclientsub.ttcocivcn.oraclevcn.com:1521/ifsread1cs_svc.int.ttc.ca

Then the generated connection string is:

Driver={Oracle in OraClient19Home1};
Dbq=ocipp-dqcof-scan.ttcociclientsub.ttcocivcn.oraclevcn.com:1521/ifsread1cs_svc.int.ttc.ca;
Uid=<login>;
Pwd=<password>;

This is preferable to hard-coding the Oracle host/service name into the DTSX.

So your environments could simply have:

DEV

IFS_ORACLE_Server_Name = dev-host:1521/dev-service

QA

IFS_ORACLE_Server_Name = qa-host:1521/qa-service

PROD

IFS_ORACLE_Server_Name = prod-host:1521/prod-service

while the DTSX remains identical.

3. CES connection

Your existing CES connection is currently an OLE DB connection using MSOLEDBSQL.1, and the destinations and Execute SQL task all reference CES_Connection.

That is good architecture.

Do not change the connection manager name.

Keep:

CES_Connection

The only change is how its ConnectionString is populated.

Likewise, keep:

IFS_Connection

because all of your Oracle ODBC source components reference that connection manager. For example, DFT_Load_PartMaster references IFS_Connection for its ODBC source.

Therefore you don't need to edit the individual Data Flow Tasks.

4. One important SSIS deployment issue

Because you are deploying to SSISDB, configure the six project parameters through the SSISDB Environment rather than embedding environment-specific values in the DTSX.

Recommended structure:

SSISDB
└── CES_IFS_Interface
    ├── Environments
    │   ├── DEV
    │   ├── QA
    │   └── PROD
    └── Projects
        └── CES_IFS_Interface
            └── Packages
                └── pkg_CES_IFS_Interface.dtsx

For example, PROD environment:

CES_SQL_Server_Name
CES_SQL_Login
CES_SQL_Password

IFS_ORACLE_Server_Name
IFS_ORACLE_Login
IFS_ORACLE_Password

The package then remains environment-independent.

5. What I intentionally did NOT change

I preserved the existing package processing logic.

The package still:

TRUNCATE_CES_Tables
        ↓
DFT_Load_VendorPartDesc
        ↓
DFT_Load_PartOrder
        ↓
DFT_Load_PartMaster
        ↓
DFT_Load_TextData

The existing precedence constraints are still present.

I also left the existing RunDate package parameter/variable untouched. The package currently has a RunDate package parameter and corresponding variable.

The existing error handler continues to use the CES connection manager, which is appropriate because the error is logged into the CES database.

6. Deployment checklist
In Visual Studio / SSDT
Open the SSIS project.
Open Project.params.

Delete:

CES_ConnectionString
IFS_ConnectionString
CES_Password
IFS_Password

Add:

CES_SQL_Server_Name
CES_SQL_Login
CES_SQL_Password

IFS_ORACLE_Server_Name
IFS_ORACLE_Login
IFS_ORACLE_Password

Mark:

CES_SQL_Password
IFS_ORACLE_Password

as Sensitive.

Replace your package with the downloaded DTSX.
Open the package in SSDT.
Verify both connection managers.
Build the .ispac.
Deploy to SSISDB.
In SSISDB

Create/configure the environment values.

For example:

CES_SQL_Server_Name       = <SQL Server name>
CES_SQL_Login             = <CES login>
CES_SQL_Password          = <CES password>

IFS_ORACLE_Server_Name    = <Oracle host:port/service>
IFS_ORACLE_Login          = <Oracle login>
IFS_ORACLE_Password       = <Oracle password>

Then create project parameter references from the environment variables.

One production recommendation

I would not use a connection string parameter anymore. Your requested six-parameter design is better:

Environment
     │
     ├── Server
     ├── Login
     └── Password
          │
          ▼
   SSIS Project Parameter
          │
          ▼
   Connection Manager
          │
          ├── CES SQL Server
          └── Oracle IFS

That gives you one .ispac that can be deployed to DEV/QA/PROD without editing the DTSX.
